  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThere really just is not much sensible weather, at least changes, to \nspeak of. Fortunately, that tends to mean pleasant overall \nconditions across the area... \n\nThe large scale mid-upper level pattern has a good amount of \nactivity within it over the coming days, just not likely to affect \nthe immediate area. Currently the pattern is highlighted by a trough-\nridge-trough pattern across CONUS with the ridge axis just a hair \nwest of the Missouri. The western CONUS trough remains expected to \nsplit with a piece riding inland and NE around the stout ridge and \nanother piece digging into southern California and becoming a cutoff \nlow. The aforementioned northeastward moving piece will yield a \ndeveloping surface low and attendant cold front by Saturday, but its \nrapid movement into Canada leaves little support for the front to \npush much across the Central Plains. This too is bolstered by the \nlagging cutoff low beginning to move further inland, preventing any \ntrailing surface high support to develop. With broad surface high \npressure remaining over the eastern CONUS, all of this is to say \nthat dry conditions and southerly winds remain through the weekend. \nWith little change in mixing depth, wind speeds, or 925-850mb temps, \nexpect highs to be quite similar to yesterday... mid to upper 70s \nfor most and a few touching 80. Of which would be upwards of 10-15+ \ndeg above normal. \n\nTo start the work week, confidence in the broad pattern evolution \nremains high within GEFS/ECMWF ENS. This can be gleaned a few ways, \nincluding within temp spreads. Both ensemble systems tend to suggest \n25th/75th spreads of ~5 deg F or less, with that strongest within \nthe ECMWF ENS through Tuesday/Wednesday. The existing mid-level \nridge will continue to keep conditions on the dry side as it \ndeflects western CONUS cutoff low (becoming an open wave). The more \nsoutherly path of this trough will more substantially flatten the \nridge and bring about an outside shot at some light precipitation \nover far W/NW areas of the CWA as early as Monday evening. Given the \npre-existing very dry air mass, it would likely take a notable \nsoutherly/easterly trend in the trough path to see measurable \nprecipitation chances. Generally expect temperatures to remain above \nnormal but likely a few degrees cooler than the weekend, in the low \nto mid 70s, with an uptick in cloud cover and slightly cooler 925-\n850mb temps. \n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance both begin to diverge more \nsignificantly mid-late week, primarily differing in their handling \nof a series of northern stream shortwaves and larger troughs/cutoff \nlows. Difficult to pin down much in the way of confidence in any \nfrontal or precipitation timing. The 'strongest' takeaways over the \nlatter half of the work week would be best chances for cooling \ntemperatures, and increasing precipitation chances.",
